WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:11.500
Imagine trying to predict a massive national election, but you're only allowed to pull

00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:13.220
the residents of a single apartment building.

00:00:13.220 --> 00:00:14.220
Right.

00:00:14.220 --> 00:00:15.220
You'd be completely off.

00:00:15.220 --> 00:00:16.220
Exactly.

00:00:16.220 --> 00:00:17.460
I mean, you would just be a little bit off.

00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:20.580
Your entire model would be totally detached from reality.

00:00:20.580 --> 00:00:26.180
You're projecting the behavior of millions based on this tiny, isolated fraction of

00:00:26.180 --> 00:00:27.180
the population.

00:00:27.180 --> 00:00:29.300
Which is a terrible way to do math, obviously.

00:00:29.300 --> 00:00:30.300
Right.

00:00:30.700 --> 00:00:34.420
Apply that same logic to how the government funds healthcare for hundreds of thousands

00:00:34.420 --> 00:00:40.020
of really vulnerable people, and you start to see the outline of this very real, very urgent

00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:41.020
crisis.

00:00:41.020 --> 00:00:43.620
Welcome to the deep dive, by the way.

00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:48.540
Today we're opening up a stack of documents detailing this escalating bureaucratic and

00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:51.660
political battle over Medicare Advantage funding in Puerto Rico.

00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:53.780
And the stakes here, I mean, they really couldn't be higher.

00:00:53.780 --> 00:00:58.020
We were looking at a whole healthcare ecosystem that functions on a mathematical framework

00:00:58.020 --> 00:01:00.940
that is, frankly, entirely counterintuitive.

00:01:00.940 --> 00:01:05.260
It's a system intacting over half a million lives, but the mechanisms driving it seem

00:01:05.260 --> 00:01:08.460
caught in this bizarre bureaucratic paradox.

00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:11.820
And we have a fascinating set of sources to guide us through this for you today.

00:01:11.820 --> 00:01:17.780
The primary catalyst is this congressional letter from August 3, 2026.

00:01:17.780 --> 00:01:24.180
It was spearheaded by Representative Dr. Greg Murphy and signed by 13 other Republicans.

00:01:24.180 --> 00:01:27.380
Sent directly to HHS Secretary Robert F. Kennedy, Jr.

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:28.420
Exactly.

00:01:28.420 --> 00:01:33.580
And we also have the official response from September 14, 2026 from CMS, the Centers for

00:01:33.580 --> 00:01:35.820
Medicare and Medicaid Services.

00:01:35.820 --> 00:01:38.980
That one was signed by Dr. Memedaz on behalf of the Secretary.

00:01:38.980 --> 00:01:44.180
Plus, we're looking at this extensive historical record documenting a decade of CMS policy

00:01:44.180 --> 00:01:45.180
actions.

00:01:45.180 --> 00:01:46.180
It's quite the paper trail.

00:01:46.180 --> 00:01:47.180
It really is.

00:01:47.180 --> 00:01:50.140
But before we get into the weeds, I do want to establish our ground rules here.

00:01:50.140 --> 00:01:54.340
These documents contain politically charged advocacy, you know, and really robust

00:01:54.340 --> 00:01:55.340
government defenses.

00:01:55.340 --> 00:01:56.340
Oh, absolutely.

00:01:56.340 --> 00:01:58.380
And it needs to be clear that we are totally impartial here.

00:01:58.380 --> 00:01:59.660
We aren't taking sides.

00:01:59.660 --> 00:02:02.700
We aren't endorsing any specific viewpoints left or right.

00:02:02.700 --> 00:02:06.500
Our goal is strictly to analyze the facts, the math, and the arguments laid out in the

00:02:06.500 --> 00:02:07.620
original text.

00:02:07.620 --> 00:02:11.620
Which is so necessary because underneath all that political back and forth is a really

00:02:11.620 --> 00:02:15.420
profound structural question about how data dictates human welfare.

00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:19.820
I mean, when you isolate the arithmetic from the politics, you find a formula that

00:02:19.820 --> 00:02:22.420
is incredibly difficult to defend.

00:02:22.420 --> 00:02:23.980
Just on a purely statistical level.

00:02:23.980 --> 00:02:24.980
Exactly.

00:02:24.980 --> 00:02:26.100
So let's start with the immediate standoff.

00:02:26.100 --> 00:02:31.300
We've got this August 2026 demand from Congressman Murphy and his colleagues, and then the September

00:02:31.300 --> 00:02:33.820
response from CMS.

00:02:33.820 --> 00:02:38.340
In the letter, the lawmakers are asking for a national minimum Medicare Advantage payment

00:02:38.340 --> 00:02:40.180
level for Puerto Rico.

00:02:40.180 --> 00:02:41.180
Basically a floor.

00:02:41.180 --> 00:02:42.180
Right.

00:02:42.180 --> 00:02:43.180
A benchmark floor.

00:02:43.180 --> 00:02:46.620
And they want it set at 70% of the national average for 2028.

00:02:46.620 --> 00:02:51.260
So why 70%, like what's their legal justification for demanding that specific number?

00:02:51.260 --> 00:02:53.940
Well, the 70% figure isn't just arbitrary.

00:02:53.940 --> 00:02:57.460
The lawmakers are pointing directly to the neighboring U.S. Virgin Islands.

00:02:57.460 --> 00:02:58.460
Oh, okay.

00:02:58.460 --> 00:02:59.460
Yeah.

00:02:59.460 --> 00:03:02.380
The Virgin Islands currently enjoys that 70% benchmark level.

00:03:02.380 --> 00:03:07.740
So the Republicans are arguing that under section 1876 of the Social Security Act, CMS

00:03:07.740 --> 00:03:10.980
actually has the statutory authority to treat Puerto Rico similarly.

00:03:10.980 --> 00:03:11.980
Which makes sense on paper.

00:03:11.980 --> 00:03:12.980
Right.

00:03:12.980 --> 00:03:15.660
And the gap they're trying to close is massive.

00:03:15.660 --> 00:03:20.380
Right now, Puerto Rico's Medicare Advantage benchmarks are sitting at about 41% below

00:03:20.380 --> 00:03:21.580
the national average.

00:03:21.580 --> 00:03:22.580
Yeah.

00:03:22.580 --> 00:03:23.580
41% below.

00:03:23.940 --> 00:03:29.100
And that gap, according to the letter, has just been widening continuously since 2012.

00:03:29.100 --> 00:03:32.620
So the lawmakers are essentially saying, you know, stop the bleeding.

00:03:32.620 --> 00:03:35.780
Set a concrete floor so the funding doesn't drop any further.

00:03:35.780 --> 00:03:38.180
Which brings us to the September 14 letter.

00:03:38.180 --> 00:03:41.620
Dr. Mehmet Oz responds for the administration.

00:03:41.620 --> 00:03:44.940
And he doesn't grant the 70% floor, does he?

00:03:44.940 --> 00:03:45.940
He does not.

00:03:45.940 --> 00:03:46.940
No.

00:03:46.940 --> 00:03:50.340
The response from CMS states they will, quote, take these views into account for

00:03:50.340 --> 00:03:51.980
the 2028 cycle.

00:03:51.980 --> 00:03:53.260
Standard government speak.

00:03:53.260 --> 00:03:54.460
Very much so.

00:03:54.460 --> 00:03:58.060
But they maintain that their current approach is specific to Puerto Rico and consistent with

00:03:58.060 --> 00:04:01.500
their statutory authorities and their assessment of the relevant data.

00:04:01.500 --> 00:04:05.820
So instead of granting the floor, the letter defends CMS's recent record.

00:04:05.820 --> 00:04:06.820
Defends it how?

00:04:06.820 --> 00:04:12.260
Well, by highlighting some specific incremental adjustments they've already made for the 2027

00:04:12.260 --> 00:04:13.260
calendar year.

00:04:13.260 --> 00:04:14.260
Okay.

00:04:14.260 --> 00:04:17.660
Incremental adjustments instead of a systemic fix.

00:04:17.660 --> 00:04:19.740
What kind of adjustments are we talking about here?

00:04:19.740 --> 00:04:24.380
One of the main things they highlight is an adjustment for the, quote, propensity of zero

00:04:24.380 --> 00:04:25.380
claims.

00:04:25.380 --> 00:04:26.820
Propensity of zero claims.

00:04:26.820 --> 00:04:27.820
Yeah.

00:04:27.820 --> 00:04:32.700
CMS acknowledges that beneficiaries in Puerto Rico generate zero medical claims at a much,

00:04:32.700 --> 00:04:35.700
much higher rate than the national average.

00:04:35.700 --> 00:04:39.900
So they directed the office of the actuary to bump up the calculations to

00:04:39.900 --> 00:04:41.460
reflect nationwide averages.

00:04:41.460 --> 00:04:42.460
Okay.

00:04:42.460 --> 00:04:43.460
And what did that actually do?

00:04:43.460 --> 00:04:46.100
It resulted in a 4.4% increase.

00:04:46.100 --> 00:04:50.820
They also point to a regulatory exception they made back in April, 2026.

00:04:50.820 --> 00:04:56.660
That one allowed certain dual eligible special needs plans to offer more than one plan in territories

00:04:56.660 --> 00:04:58.820
that lack standard Medicare savings programs.

00:04:58.820 --> 00:04:59.820
Hold on.

00:04:59.820 --> 00:05:01.780
I want to unpack that zero claims adjustment for a second.

00:05:01.780 --> 00:05:02.780
Yeah.

00:05:02.780 --> 00:05:06.780
Because it sounds like this really dry actuarial term, but there is real human behavior

00:05:06.780 --> 00:05:07.780
behind it, right?

00:05:07.780 --> 00:05:08.780
Oh, absolutely.

00:05:08.780 --> 00:05:12.060
Because if a population has a high propensity for zero claims, it doesn't mean they're

00:05:12.060 --> 00:05:14.820
super humanly healthy and just never need a doctor.

00:05:14.820 --> 00:05:15.820
Far from it.

00:05:15.860 --> 00:05:21.740
Actually, in a lot of underfunded healthcare markets, a high rate of zero claims is a symptom

00:05:21.740 --> 00:05:22.740
of poverty.

00:05:22.740 --> 00:05:23.980
It's a lack of access.

00:05:23.980 --> 00:05:28.820
If a senior is struggling financially and they face out-of-pocket costs or co-pays just

00:05:28.820 --> 00:05:31.660
to go see a specialist, they often simply don't go.

00:05:31.660 --> 00:05:33.340
They delay care entirely.

00:05:33.340 --> 00:05:37.580
And because they delay care, they literally don't generate a medical claim in the system.

00:05:37.580 --> 00:05:38.580
Exactly.

00:05:38.580 --> 00:05:39.580
They become invisible to the math.

00:05:39.580 --> 00:05:42.380
Which means the system looks at the data and says, oh, these people don't cost

00:05:42.380 --> 00:05:43.580
very much to treat.

00:05:43.580 --> 00:05:45.580
And then it lowers the funding even more.

00:05:45.580 --> 00:05:47.300
It is a totally vicious cycle.

00:05:47.300 --> 00:05:51.380
The poverty suppresses the data and the data justifies less funding.

00:05:51.380 --> 00:05:53.140
That is the exact trap.

00:05:53.140 --> 00:06:00.260
And CMS recognized this specific data distortion, which is why they applied that 4.4% bump.

00:06:00.260 --> 00:06:06.300
But to the 14 lawmakers writing that August letter, a 4.4% bump is basically a band-aid

00:06:06.300 --> 00:06:07.500
on a gunshot wound.

00:06:07.500 --> 00:06:08.500
Yeah.

00:06:08.500 --> 00:06:13.700
I mean, it feels like a tenant begging the landlord to fix a fundamentally cracked foundation

00:06:13.700 --> 00:06:15.460
because the house is sinking.

00:06:15.460 --> 00:06:18.760
And the landlord writes back saying, hey, we hear your concerns.

00:06:18.760 --> 00:06:22.020
And please note, we just put a fresh coat of paint on the mailbox.

00:06:22.020 --> 00:06:23.020
That's a great way to put it.

00:06:23.020 --> 00:06:26.020
I mean, the landlord isn't lying about the mailbox.

00:06:26.020 --> 00:06:27.020
Right.

00:06:27.020 --> 00:06:29.500
But they are completely ignoring the foundation.

00:06:29.500 --> 00:06:34.820
And that foundation is where the math gets truly staggering to really understand why

00:06:34.820 --> 00:06:38.940
Congressman Murphy and the others are calling this formula rigged.

00:06:38.940 --> 00:06:42.540
We have to look at the popularity of Medicare Advantage in Puerto Rico.

00:06:42.540 --> 00:06:44.060
All the numbers are wild.

00:06:44.060 --> 00:06:49.060
Over 670,000 seniors on the island are enrolled in Medicare Advantage.

00:06:49.060 --> 00:06:50.060
Yep.

00:06:50.060 --> 00:06:54.660
And according to the sources, that represents 96% of the entire Medicare eligible population

00:06:54.660 --> 00:06:55.660
there.

00:06:55.660 --> 00:06:56.660
96%.

00:06:56.660 --> 00:07:00.220
It is the highest participation rate in the entire nation by a mile.

00:07:00.220 --> 00:07:01.660
But here's the kicker.

00:07:01.660 --> 00:07:05.940
The funding formula for Medicare Advantage isn't actually based on the 96% who are

00:07:05.940 --> 00:07:06.940
in the program.

00:07:06.940 --> 00:07:08.020
No, it's not.

00:07:08.020 --> 00:07:12.860
By law, Medicare Advantage-based funding is tied to the cost of original Medicare.

00:07:12.860 --> 00:07:15.540
To traditional fee-for-service program.

00:07:15.540 --> 00:07:19.580
Exactly the FFS program in that specific geographic region.

00:07:19.580 --> 00:07:22.660
Let me stop you there, because this brings us right back to the apartment building analogy

00:07:22.660 --> 00:07:24.140
from the intro.

00:07:24.140 --> 00:07:31.900
If 96% of the seniors are in Medicare Advantage, that leaves only 4% in original Medicare.

00:07:31.900 --> 00:07:34.540
Which translates to about 29,000 people.

00:07:34.540 --> 00:07:39.900
Wait, so the budget for nearly 700,000 people is being dictated by the medical bills of just

00:07:39.900 --> 00:07:41.820
29,000 people.

00:07:41.820 --> 00:07:43.820
That's the math.

00:07:43.820 --> 00:07:49.340
Statistically, how can a 3% or 4% sample accurately capture the healthcare reality of

00:07:49.340 --> 00:07:50.340
an entire population?

00:07:50.340 --> 00:07:51.780
I mean, that makes no sense.

00:07:51.780 --> 00:07:54.220
No lawmakers argue it absolutely cannot.

00:07:54.220 --> 00:07:58.980
The Murphy letter explicitly states that 29,000 beneficiaries is just way too small

00:07:58.980 --> 00:08:02.220
a sample to reflect the true cost of a functional program.

00:08:02.220 --> 00:08:04.020
The data pool is incredibly shallow.

00:08:04.020 --> 00:08:05.180
It actually worked.

00:08:05.180 --> 00:08:09.100
And this distortion is magnified when you look at the unique vulnerabilities of this specific

00:08:09.100 --> 00:08:13.300
population because Puerto Rico doesn't have the same safety nets as the mainland.

00:08:13.300 --> 00:08:16.740
Right, they are missing massive pieces of the federal support structure.

00:08:16.740 --> 00:08:17.740
They really are.

00:08:17.740 --> 00:08:19.660
They don't have the Medicare Savings Program.

00:08:19.660 --> 00:08:22.140
They lack long-term services and supports.

00:08:22.140 --> 00:08:25.060
They are excluded from supplemental security income.

00:08:25.060 --> 00:08:28.500
And they don't even have the standard Supplemental Nutrition Assistance Program,

00:08:28.500 --> 00:08:29.500
right?

00:08:29.500 --> 00:08:30.500
SNAP.

00:08:30.500 --> 00:08:31.500
Exactly.

00:08:31.500 --> 00:08:32.820
No standard SNAP.

00:08:32.820 --> 00:08:36.220
Which completely changes the role of Medicare Advantage.

00:08:36.220 --> 00:08:40.660
If you're a low-income senior on the mainland, you have this whole web of federal programs

00:08:40.660 --> 00:08:41.660
to catch you.

00:08:41.660 --> 00:08:42.700
But if you're in Puerto Rico.

00:08:42.700 --> 00:08:48.060
If you're in Puerto Rico, and keep in mind, nearly half of these beneficiaries are dual-eligible

00:08:48.060 --> 00:08:52.500
for both Medicare and Medicaid, Medicare Advantage basically becomes the de facto

00:08:52.500 --> 00:08:53.500
safety net.

00:08:53.500 --> 00:08:55.580
It's the only thing keeping the system afloat.

00:08:55.580 --> 00:08:56.580
Right.

00:08:56.580 --> 00:09:01.380
And when your only safety net is funded by a formula based on a 3% data sample,

00:09:01.380 --> 00:09:04.700
you are perpetually starved of resources.

00:09:04.700 --> 00:09:06.700
And this isn't some new realization either.

00:09:06.700 --> 00:09:07.700
No.

00:09:07.700 --> 00:09:11.860
If we look at the historical document in the sources, we see a 12-year record of stakeholders

00:09:11.860 --> 00:09:13.900
pointing out this exact flaw.

00:09:13.900 --> 00:09:18.740
From calendar year 2016, all the way to 2027, there has been this continuous push on

00:09:18.740 --> 00:09:20.940
the administrative record for a benchmark floor.

00:09:20.940 --> 00:09:23.460
12 years of asking for the exact same thing.

00:09:23.460 --> 00:09:27.740
So if the formula is this obviously broken, and people have been flagging it for

00:09:27.740 --> 00:09:31.420
over a decade, how is CMS justified ignoring it?

00:09:31.420 --> 00:09:34.100
Have they just been giving the same denial every year?

00:09:34.100 --> 00:09:35.420
Well, no, that's the interesting part.

00:09:35.420 --> 00:09:41.540
The historical record shows that CMS's rationale for refusing the floor has constantly migrated.

00:09:41.540 --> 00:09:42.540
Migrated how?

00:09:42.540 --> 00:09:47.620
So in 2016, they claimed that setting a minimum benchmark simply wasn't permissible under

00:09:47.620 --> 00:09:48.620
statute.

00:09:48.620 --> 00:09:50.540
They said they didn't have the authority.

00:09:50.540 --> 00:09:55.260
But then by 2017 and 2018, the reasoning shifted entirely.

00:09:55.260 --> 00:09:56.260
Let me guess.

00:09:56.260 --> 00:10:01.580
They started arguing that the 3% fee for service data actually was sufficient to establish accurate

00:10:01.580 --> 00:10:02.580
benchmarks.

00:10:02.580 --> 00:10:06.660
Wait, so they flip-flopped from we don't have the legal power to actually the data

00:10:06.660 --> 00:10:07.660
is perfectly fine?

00:10:07.660 --> 00:10:09.460
It gets even more confusing.

00:10:09.460 --> 00:10:13.980
In 2019, they deferred entirely, saying they would just, quote, continue to analyze

00:10:13.980 --> 00:10:14.980
the issue.

00:10:14.980 --> 00:10:19.500
Then, from 2020 through 2024, they leaned on this dual argument, saying they had

00:10:19.500 --> 00:10:22.540
to follow the local data and claiming there was no evidence that costs should

00:10:22.540 --> 00:10:23.540
be higher.

00:10:23.540 --> 00:10:24.540
Unbelievable.

00:10:24.540 --> 00:10:30.700
Finally, in the most recent cycles, 2025 to 2027, they adopted a totally new phrasing,

00:10:30.700 --> 00:10:34.540
stating they have limited discretion to incorporate exceptions.

00:10:34.540 --> 00:10:38.340
So they act like the rulebook is written in stone, but they just keep swapping out the

00:10:38.340 --> 00:10:39.340
stone.

00:10:39.340 --> 00:10:40.340
It's incredibly evasive.

00:10:40.340 --> 00:10:44.100
But what about that statutory authority the lawmaker has mentioned, the part about

00:10:44.100 --> 00:10:46.260
treating Puerto Rico like the Virgin Islands?

00:10:46.260 --> 00:10:47.260
Right.

00:10:47.260 --> 00:10:50.260
That is section 1876A4.

00:10:50.260 --> 00:10:53.220
It's often referred to as the similar area clause.

00:10:53.220 --> 00:10:57.220
It essentially says that if you don't have good data for one area, you can use a similar

00:10:57.220 --> 00:10:58.460
area as a proxy.

00:10:58.460 --> 00:11:00.700
Which makes perfect sense for a 3% data pool.

00:11:00.700 --> 00:11:02.100
You'd think.

00:11:02.100 --> 00:11:06.300
But the historical record points out that despite stakeholders explicitly pressing this

00:11:06.300 --> 00:11:12.220
specific clause upon CMS in 2024, CMS has consistently failed to even address that

00:11:12.220 --> 00:11:14.420
specific text in their refusals.

00:11:14.420 --> 00:11:18.780
I have to challenge this premise that CMS has, quote, limited discretion because

00:11:18.780 --> 00:11:21.780
earlier you mentioned Dr. Oz highlighting adjustments they did make.

00:11:21.780 --> 00:11:25.340
I mean, if their hands are truly tied by the statute, how are they making those other

00:11:25.340 --> 00:11:26.340
changes?

00:11:26.340 --> 00:11:29.940
And this is exactly what the proven document in our sources explores.

00:11:29.940 --> 00:11:34.540
It details four major instances where CMS found the administrative authority to make

00:11:34.540 --> 00:11:38.300
significant changes without needing an act of Congress.

00:11:38.300 --> 00:11:39.820
So they can do it when they want to.

00:11:39.820 --> 00:11:40.820
Clearly.

00:11:40.820 --> 00:11:46.300
For instance, in 2012, CMS restricted the baseline calculation to only include beneficiaries

00:11:46.300 --> 00:11:49.380
who had both part A and part B of Medicare.

00:11:49.380 --> 00:11:54.220
They recognized that Puerto Rico's auto-enrollment rules were skewing the data, so they administratively

00:11:54.220 --> 00:11:55.340
chose to filter it.

00:11:55.340 --> 00:11:56.940
They just filtered the data on their own.

00:11:56.940 --> 00:11:57.940
Yep.

00:11:57.940 --> 00:12:02.620
And then in 2018, they made this massive administrative move regarding quality bonuses.

00:12:02.620 --> 00:12:07.980
They essentially reinterpreted a statute to classify 52 out of 78 municipalities in

00:12:07.980 --> 00:12:12.100
Puerto Rico as double bonus counties for star-rated plants.

00:12:12.100 --> 00:12:13.100
Wow.

00:12:13.100 --> 00:12:16.900
They found a way to read the existing law creatively to inject more funding into

00:12:16.900 --> 00:12:18.260
those specific areas.

00:12:18.260 --> 00:12:21.300
So that is the literal definition of administrative discretion.

00:12:21.300 --> 00:12:22.300
Yeah.

00:12:22.300 --> 00:12:26.500
They're finding ways to creatively interpret the statute to grant these temporary adjustments.

00:12:26.500 --> 00:12:31.020
But when 14 lawmakers ask for a permanent 70 percent floor, suddenly their hands are

00:12:31.020 --> 00:12:32.220
magically tied.

00:12:32.220 --> 00:12:34.020
They suddenly have limited discretion, right?

00:12:34.020 --> 00:12:37.980
It's like a referee who only aggressively enforces the rulebook when it penalizes

00:12:37.980 --> 00:12:42.540
the visiting team, but totally swallows the whistle for the home team.

00:12:42.540 --> 00:12:44.980
The documents point out this exact contradiction.

00:12:44.980 --> 00:12:48.140
It's basically an administrative cat and mouse game.

00:12:48.140 --> 00:12:53.300
CMS claims they have the authority for bandaids, but claims limited discretion when asked for

00:12:53.300 --> 00:12:54.900
systemic fixes.

00:12:54.900 --> 00:12:59.100
Which really brings it to the urgency of this August 2026 letter.

00:12:59.100 --> 00:13:03.060
If this cat and mouse game has been going on for 12 years, why did these lawmakers

00:13:03.060 --> 00:13:05.060
draw line in the sand right now?

00:13:05.060 --> 00:13:10.580
Like why is the 2028 rate announcement the hill they are willing to die on?

00:13:10.580 --> 00:13:13.940
Because there is a very real mechanical threat looming, and it revolves around

00:13:13.940 --> 00:13:16.180
something called the wage index cut.

00:13:16.180 --> 00:13:20.660
The 2028 rates are about to lock in a completely devastating reduction in funding.

00:13:20.660 --> 00:13:21.660
Okay.

00:13:21.660 --> 00:13:23.180
Stop right there and translate that for me.

00:13:23.180 --> 00:13:26.820
What exactly is a wage index, and how does it blow a hole in the budget?

00:13:26.820 --> 00:13:27.820
Sure.

00:13:27.820 --> 00:13:29.860
So a wage index is essentially a multiplier.

00:13:29.860 --> 00:13:34.020
CMS uses it to adjust health care payments based on the local cost of labor.

00:13:34.020 --> 00:13:37.780
You know, it costs more to hire a nurse in Manhattan than it does in rural Nebraska,

00:13:37.780 --> 00:13:38.780
right?

00:13:38.780 --> 00:13:39.780
Right.

00:13:39.780 --> 00:13:41.900
So the government adjusts the payments accordingly.

00:13:41.900 --> 00:13:48.140
But currently, Puerto Rico is facing a legally locked in 5% per year step down in its applied

00:13:48.140 --> 00:13:49.460
hospital wage index.

00:13:49.460 --> 00:13:52.700
A mandated 5% cut every year.

00:13:52.700 --> 00:13:54.460
What does that look like in real numbers?

00:13:54.460 --> 00:13:57.740
Well, the sources provide the specific math for San Juan.

00:13:57.740 --> 00:14:02.580
In fiscal year 2024, the wage index for San Juan was 0.6134.

00:14:02.580 --> 00:14:06.260
But by fiscal year 2027, it had plummeted to 0.5259.

00:14:06.260 --> 00:14:10.380
Wait, a drop from 0.61 to 0.52?

00:14:10.380 --> 00:14:13.900
In real terms, that means the government is essentially declaring that the value of a health

00:14:13.900 --> 00:14:18.340
care worker's labor in San Juan just dropped by over 14%.

00:14:18.340 --> 00:14:21.260
Accumulative 14.26% drop, to be exact.

00:14:21.260 --> 00:14:25.180
And keep in mind, this index applies to 62% of the total payment, which represents the

00:14:25.180 --> 00:14:26.980
labor-related share of health care costs.

00:14:26.980 --> 00:14:27.980
That is brutal.

00:14:27.980 --> 00:14:28.980
It gets worse.

00:14:28.980 --> 00:14:32.420
The documents project that this downward grind will continue through approximately

00:14:32.420 --> 00:14:37.860
fiscal year 2035, eventually dropping the index down to roughly 0.35.

00:14:37.860 --> 00:14:40.420
That is just a staggering loss of value.

00:14:40.420 --> 00:14:44.620
But wait, if this is a hospital wage index that dropped in 2027, why are the lawmakers

00:14:44.620 --> 00:14:47.620
panicking about the Medicare Advantage rates for 2028?

00:14:47.620 --> 00:14:50.380
Ah, because of the way the data lags.

00:14:50.380 --> 00:14:52.980
It's a lot like seeing a flash of lightning embracing for the thunder.

00:14:52.980 --> 00:14:54.060
Okay, I'm following.

00:14:54.060 --> 00:14:57.500
The flash happened in 2027 when the wage index dropped.

00:14:57.500 --> 00:15:01.660
But the thunder clap, the actual cut to the Medicare Advantage benchmarks, takes

00:15:01.660 --> 00:15:02.780
a year to hit.

00:15:02.780 --> 00:15:03.920
Why a year?

00:15:03.920 --> 00:15:08.320
Because the MA benchmarks rely on five years of fee for service claims.

00:15:08.320 --> 00:15:12.560
Before CMS calculates the averages, they reprice that historical data using the

00:15:12.560 --> 00:15:13.560
latest parameter.

00:15:13.560 --> 00:15:14.560
Oh, I see.

00:15:14.560 --> 00:15:19.600
Meaning that massive 14% devaluation of labor from 2027 gets completely baked into

00:15:19.600 --> 00:15:21.000
the math for 2028.

00:15:21.000 --> 00:15:22.000
Exactly.

00:15:22.000 --> 00:15:23.760
The thunder clap hits in 2028.

00:15:23.760 --> 00:15:26.880
The cuts aren't just some theoretical future possibility.

00:15:26.880 --> 00:15:28.960
The arithmetic is already processed and done.

00:15:28.960 --> 00:15:29.960
Wow.

00:15:29.960 --> 00:15:34.560
CMS admitted in their 2027 announcement that these wage index policies, quote, will

00:15:34.560 --> 00:15:38.320
contribute to declines in the MA rates for Puerto Rico.

00:15:38.320 --> 00:15:42.600
So the 14 lawmakers are desperately trying to get the administration to establish the

00:15:42.600 --> 00:15:47.360
70% floor before the 2028 rates lock in those devastating cuts.

00:15:47.360 --> 00:15:51.040
It sounds like we are looking at a system in absolute gridlock.

00:15:51.040 --> 00:15:53.560
Let's pull all these threads together for you listening.

00:15:53.560 --> 00:15:58.760
We've got over 670,000 seniors living in a territory that is already missing

00:15:58.760 --> 00:16:01.000
vital federal safety nets.

00:16:01.000 --> 00:16:05.000
96% of them have chosen Medicare Advantage, making it the highest

00:16:05.000 --> 00:16:08.840
participating, highest performing market of its kind in the entire nation.

00:16:08.840 --> 00:16:09.840
Absolutely.

00:16:09.840 --> 00:16:13.360
Yet their funding is trapped by this totally warped formula, relying on the

00:16:13.360 --> 00:16:16.240
medical bills of just 29,000 people.

00:16:16.240 --> 00:16:20.960
They are facing a locked in decade-long series of wage cuts that actively

00:16:20.960 --> 00:16:23.120
devalue their healthcare workers.

00:16:23.120 --> 00:16:26.840
And when lawmakers demand a permanent floor based on a nearby territory,

00:16:26.840 --> 00:16:30.280
the government defends its use of temporary administrative band-aids.

00:16:30.280 --> 00:16:33.920
It is a scenario where the internal logic of the funding model has entirely

00:16:33.920 --> 00:16:36.920
divorced itself from the reality of the healthcare environment it's actually

00:16:36.920 --> 00:16:38.120
supposed to support.

00:16:38.120 --> 00:16:42.600
Which leaves us with a really profound final thought to mull over.

00:16:42.600 --> 00:16:46.120
We've spent a lot of time today on the math and the history, but step

00:16:46.120 --> 00:16:49.520
back and consider the principles of our republic.

00:16:49.520 --> 00:16:52.520
In our system of government, we operate on the belief that we are

00:16:52.520 --> 00:16:55.400
all measured by results and merit.

00:16:55.400 --> 00:16:59.680
We design systems to reward efficiency, quality, and high adoption.

00:16:59.680 --> 00:17:00.680
That's the ideal.

00:17:00.680 --> 00:17:01.680
Right.

00:17:01.680 --> 00:17:02.680
Right.

00:17:02.680 --> 00:17:05.480
So imagine a scenario in a system supposedly built on merit, where the

00:17:05.480 --> 00:17:09.000
single market that overwhelmingly embraces that system, achieving an

00:17:09.000 --> 00:17:14.320
astonishing 96% participation rate, is systematically penalized with the

00:17:14.320 --> 00:17:17.600
lowest funding in the nation simply because it's an outlier.

00:17:17.600 --> 00:17:20.120
The math actively punishes their success.

00:17:20.120 --> 00:17:21.120
Exactly.

00:17:21.120 --> 00:17:23.400
The lawmakers and our sources are making a really strong

00:17:23.400 --> 00:17:27.440
recommendation to the administration to start penalizing the best

00:17:27.440 --> 00:17:29.640
performing market in the whole nation.

00:17:29.640 --> 00:17:32.160
They're urging Secretary Kennedy to use his administrative

00:17:32.160 --> 00:17:34.880
authority to fix a rigged formula.

00:17:34.880 --> 00:17:37.960
Because if the absolute best, most outstanding market is being rewarded

00:17:37.960 --> 00:17:41.440
as the worst, doesn't that make us pause and think that there is

00:17:41.440 --> 00:17:44.760
something completely wrong in the way we measure healthcare?

00:17:44.760 --> 00:17:45.760
It absolutely should.

00:17:45.760 --> 00:17:48.600
If the best results are being reimbursed the worst, how can we

00:17:48.600 --> 00:17:53.080
expect the worst, which is being reimbursed the best, to ever excel?

00:17:53.080 --> 00:17:55.800
This is the ultimate healthcare paradox.

00:17:55.800 --> 00:17:59.400
When strict adherence to a broken formula overrides common sense, it is no

00:17:59.400 --> 00:18:01.360
